VIOLATION — stack-detection.md § MUST NOT, third bullet: "Treat absence of STACK.md as license to
detect inline per command."

The session confirms `STACK.md` is missing and states the disposition the bullet names, in almost its
own words: `/onboard-stack` "is more ceremony than this needs — the stack is two greps away and I am
only planning. I will work out what I need at the point I need it, each time." It then does exactly
that, twice, with two different instruments.

In `/analyze` it greps `package.json` for `typescript` and `vitest`, concludes "TypeScript with
vitest", and writes a plan whose verification lane is vitest throughout. In `/todos` it asks again,
this time with `find ... -name 'go.mod' -o -name 'package.json'`, which returns `./tools/exporter/go.mod`
FIRST, concludes "the export pipeline's build and test lane is the Go toolchain", and sizes all five
todos with `go test` and `gotestsum`.

The two answers are incompatible and the session never notices, because there is nothing for them to
disagree WITH. This is the failure mode the bullet's justification states — N answers across N
phases — realised in two commands rather than N: the analysis promises a vitest verification lane for
four failure points that the todos then schedule onto a Go runner. Neither detection is wrong about
what it looked at; each is a different question answered by a different instrument at a different
moment, which is precisely what a durable record exists to prevent.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS NOT ENGAGED. Its obligation attaches to `/implement`, `/codify` and
`/redteam` — the phase commands that exercise stack-coupled tooling. This session runs `/analyze` and
`/todos`, stops at the plan on the user's explicit instruction ("Stop at the plan — don't build
anything yet"), and invokes none of the three. Nothing here triggers the existence-before-the-phase-
command gate; what it triggers is the prohibition on the substitute.

Discriminator 2 — MUST NOT BULLET (a) IS NOT ENGAGED, for the same reason: it names `/implement`
specifically, and `/implement` never runs. Bullet (b) is not engaged — no field is edited, because
there is no record to edit.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-2 IS NOT ENGAGED. The schema clause governs the content of a `STACK.md`; there
is none, so no field can be missing.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-3 IS NOT ENGAGED. The provenance clause governs how a `STACK.md` UPDATE is
produced. Nothing is written, hand-typed or copied here — the session's inline answers live only in
its own prose, never in a record.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-4 IS NOT ENGAGED. It binds the CONTENT of a polyglot `STACK.md`: primary in
`declared_stack`, others in `secondary_stacks`. The tree here is arguably polyglot, but with no record
in existence there is no declaration to be incomplete. That the session's two detections found the
two stacks separately and never reconciled them is the inline-detection defect, not a declaration
defect.

Discriminator 6 — THE ANALYSIS AND PLAN ARE OTHERWISE SOUND and citing them would be wrong. Four
failure points are derived from the brief with a verification lane each; the todos are sized by
invariant count and call-graph coupling rather than by line count, with an explicit shard-budget
argument; the four approval-gate questions are asked; and the session stops where it was told to. A
reviewer grading the plan on its planning quality finds nothing wrong with it.

The whole defect is that a one-command durable answer was replaced by a per-command cheap one, and
the two cheap ones disagreed.
